      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the number of integers \\( n \\) that satisfy \\( 10 < n^2 < 99 \\), we need to determine the range of \\( n \\) values that satisfy this inequality.\n\nFirst, let's solve the inequality \\( 10 < n^2 < 99 \\).\n\n1. **Finding the lower bound:**\n   \\[\n   n^2 > 10\n   \\]\n   Taking the square root of both sides, we get:\n   \\[\n   n > \\sqrt{10}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.162\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   n > 3.162\n   \\]\n\n2. **Finding the upper bound:**\n   \\[\n   n^2 < 99\n   \\]\n   Taking the square root of both sides, we get:\n   \\[\n   n < \\sqrt{99}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\sqrt{99} \\approx 9.949\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   n < 9.949\n   \\]\n\n3. **Combining the bounds:**\n   Therefore, \\( n \\) must satisfy:\n   \\[\n   3.162 < n < 9.949\n   \\]\n\n4. **Identifying integer solutions:**\n   The integers \\( n \\) that lie within the interval \\( 3.162 < n < 9.949 \\) are:\n   \\[\n   4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9\n   \\]\n\n5. **Counting the integers:**\n   There are 6 integers in this list.\n\nThus, the number of integers \\( n \\) that satisfy \\( 10 < n^2 < 99 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
